# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Why a Patio's Base Doesn't Need a Driveway's Depth",
     "<p>A patio never carries a car's rolling weight, which is why paver base guidance calls for at least 4 inches of compacted aggregate on well-drained soil under a patio or walkway, against 6 inches under a residential driveway. Florida's residential code still treats the ground under a {city} patio the same as any other slab on the ground: fill compacted for uniform support, topped with at least 4 inches of clean graded sand, gravel or crushed stone. On flatwoods soils like Myakka, Smyrna or EauGallie, which hold a water table within 18 inches of the surface part of the year, that lighter design load doesn't make the base work optional, only right-sized for what will actually sit on it.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Under a Patio Still Needs Shallow Lifts",
     "<p>The sand ridge soils under parts of {city}, Candler, Astatula and Tavares among them, drain so well that the water table can sit more than 80 inches down, yet that same looseness means a compactor's plate can push material sideways instead of locking it in place. Under a patio, where the base only needs to carry foot traffic and outdoor furniture rather than a vehicle, it is still worth wetting loose ridge sand enough to bind it and compacting in shallow lifts rather than one deep pass. Skipping that step on a sandy lot shows up later as a soft spot under one corner of the slab, often near wherever foot traffic concentrates, like a step down from a door.</p>"),
    ("Footers for a Lanai Go in Before the Patio Slab",
     "<p>A lanai or screen enclosure attached to a new patio needs its own footings for the support posts, sized and poured as a separate structural element from the flatwork around them. Several {city}-area permit categories draw exactly that line: a plain patio slab without footers often clears a lighter review than a slab with footers, because adding footings changes what the job is in the eyes of the permitting office. Practically, that means the footer holes get excavated, formed and poured before the surrounding patio base goes in, not worked in afterward around a finished slab. Treating the footers as an afterthought is how a screen enclosure crew shows up to a patio that isn't ready for posts.</p>"),
    ("Grading a Patio to Fall Away From the Back Door",
     "<p>A patio built right off the back of a house has to fall away from the threshold the same way a driveway falls away from a garage, even though the distances involved are much shorter. Florida's requirement that fill be compacted for uniform support applies here too, and getting the grade right sometimes means building up the outer edge of a {city} patio slightly more than the edge against the house, a difference that is rarely visible to the eye but matters every time it rains. On flatwoods lots where the water table already sits close to the surface for part of the year, a patio that drains toward the house instead of away from it turns an ordinary storm into standing water at the threshold.</p>"),
    ("A Downspout Can Undermine a Base Before It's Even Poured",
     "<p>A downspout that empties straight onto the footprint of a planned {city} patio is a base problem before it is ever a finish problem. Florida's rainy season pushes real volume through a gutter system, Orlando averages about 51.45 inches of rain a year and Sarasota-Bradenton about 49.05, and a corner of subgrade that gets soaked every time it rains compacts differently than the rest of the pad around it. Extending the downspout, adding a splash block or routing water to a swale before the base goes down costs little compared with finding out later that one corner of the patio settled because it never had a fair chance to compact evenly with the rest.</p>"),
    ("Why an Outdoor Kitchen Pad Gets Its Own Base Work",
     "<p>An outdoor kitchen pad concentrates real weight, a masonry island, a counter slab, built-in appliances, on a footprint much smaller than the patio around it, closer to how a boat trailer loads a narrow strip of driveway than to how foot traffic spreads across an open slab. We give that section of a {city} patio's base extra attention during compaction rather than treating the whole pour as one uniform area, because a soft spot under a fixed, permanently loaded island does not get a chance to redistribute the way an occasionally moved chair or table would. Planning that before the pour is simpler than diagnosing a settled corner under a grill island years later.</p>"),
    ("Matching Elevation Where a New Patio Meets an Old Slab",
     "<p>Extending a patio off an existing slab means building new fill right up against ground that has already settled and compacted for years, sometimes decades, under the old concrete. Matching the new subgrade's elevation and compaction level to the old slab's edge in {city} keeps the two sections from settling at different rates once both carry weight again, a different challenge than grading a brand-new patio on undisturbed ground. Where the old slab sits on soil the crew cannot fully see or test without breaking it out, that uncertainty gets planned for rather than assumed away, especially on flatwoods lots where an old edge may be masking a wetter spot underneath it the whole time.</p>"),
    ("A Shaded Footprint Dries Slower Than the Rest of the Yard",
     "<p>A patio footprint shaded most of the day, whether by the house, an existing lanai roof, or a stand of mature trees, dries more slowly after both compaction and rain than an open, sun-exposed slab area would. On a {city} lot where the base work falls during the rainy season, that slower drying changes the timeline more than the soil series does: a shaded corner may still be too damp to compact properly a day after an open section of the same yard is ready. Planning extra dry time, or improving drainage under the shaded section before the pour, keeps that slower-drying ground from becoming the one soft spot in an otherwise solid base.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("A Patio's Thickness Starts at Code, Not Above It",
     "<p>Florida's residential code sets 3 1/2 inches as the floor for any slab on the ground, but a patio built to that bare minimum leaves little margin for a loaded planter, a nearby footing or a few years of settling. We build a {city} patio to 4 inches, in the same general 3,000 to 4,000 psi range used for other residential flatwork, even though the slab will never see a car's weight the way a driveway does. The extra margin over code is not about matching a driveway's spec, it is about giving an exterior slab enough reserve that normal patio loads, furniture, planters, a grill, do not concentrate stress right at the code minimum.</p>"),
    ("Thickening the Slab Under a Built-In Grill Island",
     "<p>A built-in grill island, counter or pizza oven sitting in one spot for the life of the patio calls for more than the standard 4-inch section under it, the same logic that pushes a driveway from 4 to 5 or 6 inches wherever it has to carry a boat trailer's wheels. On a {city} patio, we thicken or reinforce that section locally rather than building the whole slab to the heavier spec, since the rest of the pad only ever sees foot traffic and movable furniture. Deciding where that heavier section goes has to happen at layout time, because adding a grill island to a finished patio means cutting into concrete that was not built for it.</p>"),
    ("A Lanai's Footers Aren't Sized Like the Slab Around Them",
     "<p>A screen enclosure's support posts need footings sized for that structure, not for the patio slab around them, and several {city}-area permitting offices draw a formal line between a plain slab and a slab with footers for exactly that reason. The footer's depth and width come from what the enclosure manufacturer or an engineer specifies for the posts, independent of the 4-inch flatwork thickness used for the rest of the patio. Pouring the footers as their own element, rather than trying to build extra thickness into the surrounding slab and call it enough, is what lets the posts carry wind and structural loads the slab itself was never designed to take.</p>"),
    ("Routing a Control Joint Around a Column Instead of Through It",
     "<p>Control joints on an open slab can run in a simple grid, but a lanai's support columns break that grid up, and a joint that would otherwise cross right through a post has to be routed around it instead. Panel-shape guidance still applies at every column: each section stays close to square, with the length no more than about one and a half times the width, rather than tapering into a long sliver next to a post. On a {city} patio with four or more columns, that means planning the joint layout around the columns before the pour, not sawing a standard grid afterward and hoping it lines up.</p>"),
    ("Why a Tie-In Seam Gets an Isolation Joint, Not a Bond",
     "<p>Where a new patio section ties into an existing slab, we use an isolation joint between the two rather than bonding fresh concrete directly to old, since the new pour will shrink as it cures while the existing slab already finished shrinking years ago. On a {city} tie-in, that joint, typically a strip of compressible material between the two edges, lets each section expand, contract and settle on its own schedule instead of fighting the other one at a single hard seam. Skipping the isolation joint and pouring tight against the old edge is how a tie-in develops a crack right at the seam within the first year or two.</p>"),
    ("Broom, Exposed Aggregate or Stain: Decide Before the Truck Arrives",
     "<p>A broom finish is the standard surface for a {city} patio: dragging a broom across the concrete while it is still workable leaves a texture that sheds water and resists slipping around a pool or outdoor kitchen. Exposed aggregate washes or blasts the surface cement away so the stone in the mix shows through, changing the look and the grip underfoot, while an integral color or acid stain changes the surface's appearance without changing the mix's strength. All three choices have to be locked in before the pour starts, since a plain broom finish cannot become exposed aggregate or a stained surface afterward without resurfacing the whole patio.</p>"),
    ("The Vapor Retarder Question an Open-Air Patio Raises",
     "<p>Code requires a 6-mil polyethylene vapor retarder under most slabs on the ground, with exceptions carved out for garages and unheated accessory structures, a rule written mainly with conditioned interior floors in mind. An open-air {city} patio is not conditioned living space, but it is not quite a garage or accessory structure either, so whether it falls on one side of that exception or the other is worth confirming with the local building official rather than assuming either answer. Getting it wrong in either direction, skipping a retarder code actually requires, or adding one where it serves no purpose, is an easy mistake to avoid by just asking before the pour.</p>"),
    ("Weathering Class Changes the Psi a Covered Lanai Needs",
     "<p>Florida's residential code sets different minimum strengths for exterior flatwork depending on weathering exposure, 2,500 psi at the lightest category, climbing to 3,000 and 3,500 psi as exposure gets more severe, the same table that governs porches and carports. Which category a {city} patio falls into, open to the sky versus covered by a lanai roof, is not something we assume from the job site; it is a classification the local building official applies, and getting the psi right starts with confirming that classification rather than defaulting to whichever number sounds safest. A patio built to a higher strength than its exposure calls for is not a mistake, but it is money spent on margin nobody required.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Why Footer Inspections Happen Before the Slab Pour",
     "<p>Where a {city} patio includes footings for a lanai or screen enclosure, those footers get excavated, formed and typically inspected before the surrounding patio base and slab go in, not folded into the same pour as an afterthought. That sequencing matters because a footer inspector needs to see the hole, the forms and any reinforcement before concrete covers them, the same logic that applies to a driveway's base inspection, just for a different structural element. Scheduling the footer work as its own step, ahead of grading the rest of the patio, keeps the whole job from stalling while an inspection gets arranged around concrete that has already been poured over.</p>"),
    ("Tying Into an Existing Patio Is a Two-Day Job",
     "<p>Extending an existing {city} patio is not a single pour: the first day usually goes to breaking out or grinding the old slab's edge so new concrete has a clean, known surface to tie into, and grading the new section's subgrade to match. Only once that transition edge is prepped and the forms are set does the new concrete get ordered for a second visit, rather than trying to do demolition, pour prep and finishing all in one day. Treating the tie-in edge as its own step, rather than rushing straight to the truck, is what keeps the new section's elevation and joint layout lined up with the slab it is meeting.</p>"),
    ("Starting Early When the House Itself Blocks the Morning Shade",
     "<p>A patio built close to the back of a {city} house often sits in the house's own shadow through mid-morning, which shifts the riskiest window for a hot pour later in the day than it would be on an open driveway facing the street. Hot-weather guidance still calls for avoiding the hottest stretch of the day and starting early enough that finishing and the first hours of curing happen before heat and evaporation peak, but starting early on a shaded patio sometimes means watching the sun angle as much as the clock. Reading the site's actual shade pattern, not just a generic start time, is part of planning the pour day.</p>"),
    ("Scheduling a Cure Around a Screen Enclosure Crew",
     "<p>When a screen enclosure contractor is scheduled to anchor posts into a new {city} patio soon after the pour, the curing timeline has to work backward from that install date rather than following a fixed default. Hot-weather curing calls for keeping the surface moist, by compound or wet curing, for at least 3 days before anyone treats the slab as finished work, and anchoring posts into concrete that has not had that time can disturb a cure that is still gaining strength. Coordinating the pour date with the enclosure crew's schedule, instead of booking both independently, avoids a scramble where one trade is waiting on concrete that genuinely is not ready yet.</p>"),
    ("Why a Backyard Pour Sometimes Needs a Pump Truck",
     "<p>A driveway sits right at the street, where a ready-mix truck can back up and chute concrete straight into the forms, but a {city} patio tucked behind a fence, a pool cage or a narrow side yard often cannot be reached that way at all. On jobs like that, the truck parks at the curb and a concrete pump delivers the mix through a hose to wherever the forms are, a different piece of equipment and a different scheduling conversation than a straightforward driveway pour. Confirming access before pour day, not after the truck is already on site, is what keeps a tight backyard from turning into a delay nobody planned for.</p>"),
    ("When a Patio's Paperwork Crosses the Notice of Commencement Line",
     "<p>A direct contract over $2,500 requires a Notice of Commencement recorded and posted before work starts, and a modest {city} patio poured on open ground can sometimes land under that line on its own. Add a lanai's footers, a larger footprint or an outdoor kitchen's built-in features, though, and the same job can cross both the $2,500 lien-law threshold and the $5,000 mark where the permitting authority wants a copy of the notice filed before the first inspection. Scoping the paperwork alongside the design, rather than after a contract is signed, keeps a patio project from hitting a recording requirement nobody budgeted time for.</p>"),
    ("Permit-Free Patios Skip a Step Footer Patios Can't",
     "<p>Several jurisdictions across the service area treat an on-grade {city} patio without footers as work that does not need a building permit at all, while the same patio with a lanai's footings added crosses into slab-with-footer territory that does. That distinction changes the whole front end of the process: a footer-free patio can sometimes go straight from design to pour, while a footed one needs plans submitted, a footer inspection scheduled and a final sign-off before anyone calls the job finished. Finding out which category a given {city} patio falls into, before quoting a timeline, is what keeps the schedule realistic from the first conversation.</p>"),
    ("Curing Has to Wait on the Finish You Picked",
     "<p>Curing a patio that is going to be stained or colored cannot follow the same fixed schedule as a plain gray slab, because some stains and sealers need the surface moisture content and cure time further along than a basic curing compound requires before they will take evenly. On a {city} job, that means the finish decision made back at the planning stage, broom, exposed aggregate, color, feeds directly into how the crew schedules curing and when the next trade is allowed back on the slab. Rushing a stained finish onto a patio that has not cured long enough is a common way to end up with blotchy, uneven color instead of the look that was ordered.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("Adding a Lanai Can Bump a Patio Into a Different Permit",
     "<p>A homeowner who has heard that {city} allows an on-grade patio without a building permit can be caught off guard when adding a lanai or screen enclosure later, since the footings for that structure often push the same patio into a different permit category entirely. What looked like simple flatwork now needs a footer inspection and a formal submission, and starting the posts' footings before anyone checks which category applies is how a backyard project ends up with a stop-work order partway through. Confirming the permit path before footers go in the ground, not after, is the difference between a short delay and tearing out concrete that has already cured.</p>"),
    ("A Patio Sloped Toward the House Finds the Sliding Door First",
     "<p>A patio that slopes even slightly toward the house instead of away from it sends every storm straight at whatever is closest, a sliding glass door, a lanai threshold, a foundation wall, rather than out into the yard where it belongs. On flatwoods soils already holding water near the surface for part of the year, a {city} patio with the wrong fall does not just puddle, it keeps that water sitting against the house through an entire rainy season instead of one storm. The fix almost always traces back to grading decided at the subgrade stage, not the finish, which is why checking the fall before the forms go up matters more than checking it after the pour.</p>"),
    ("A Fenced Backyard Changes How a Hot Pour Dries",
     "<p>A fenced backyard or a patio boxed in by a pool cage and tall landscaping can trap heat and cut the wind that would normally help a hot pour evaporate at a predictable rate, which changes the plastic-shrinkage math a {city} crew plans around compared with an open, wind-exposed driveway. Less wind can lower the evaporation rate, which sounds like an advantage, but a boxed-in yard also traps radiant heat from fences and walls, so the net effect on a given lot is not automatic either way. Reading the actual microclimate of a specific backyard, rather than applying the same hot-weather plan used on every job, is what keeps a shaded or enclosed pour from surprising the crew.</p>"),
    ("Two Truckloads of Colored Concrete Can Show a Seam",
     "<p>Integral color has to be batched consistently, and a {city} patio large enough to need two truckloads runs the real risk of a visible color shift where the second load meets the first, even when both loads were ordered to the same mix design. Weather, mixing time and even how long a load sits in the truck before it is placed can all nudge the shade slightly, which shows up as a faint seam across an otherwise uniform patio once it cures. Planning the pour sequence and truck timing around where that seam will fall, tucking it under a planned joint or a less visible edge, is cheaper than living with a stripe down the middle of a colored patio.</p>"),
    ("Why 'No License for Driveways' Doesn't Settle a Patio Job",
     "<p>Florida law bars local governments from requiring a license specifically for driveway installation, but patios are not named in that same carve-out, and whether a given {city} patio job needs a state-certified contractor can come down to a judgment call about whether it matches the structural masonry scope that covers slabs, footers and walls. Assuming a patio gets the same blanket exemption a driveway does, especially once footings for a lanai are part of the scope, is a mistake that surfaces at permitting, not before. Asking the local building department how it classifies a specific patio scope, rather than guessing from the driveway rule, avoids a permit application that gets kicked back.</p>"),
    ("A Downspout Corner Is Where a Base Softens First",
     "<p>A downspout left draining onto one corner of a {city} patio's footprint through construction, or relocated incorrectly after the pour, repeatedly saturates that one section of subgrade in a way the rest of the compacted base never experiences. Over a full rainy season, Orlando averages roughly 51.45 inches of rain and Sarasota-Bradenton about 49.05, that uneven wetting is exactly the kind of condition that leads to a soft spot settling unevenly while the rest of the slab stays flat. The crack or dip that shows up a year or two later usually traces straight back to that one downspout, which is why checking gutter discharge points is part of finishing the job, not an afterthought.</p>"),
    ("A Tie-In Seam Without a Plan Cracks Worse Than a Joint",
     "<p>A patio extension poured directly against an existing slab without an isolation joint, or without matching the two sections' elevation carefully, does not settle evenly as both pieces age, and the seam between old and new concrete in {city} often ends up as the single worst crack or trip edge on the whole patio. That kind of damage looks worse, and reads as a more serious problem, than the ordinary hairline cracking a slab develops along its own control joints, even though the underlying cause, two pours moving independently, was foreseeable from the start. Planning the tie-in detail before the second pour, not patching the seam after it cracks, is the cheaper path by a wide margin.</p>"),
    ("A Shaded Patio Can Discolor Before the First Year Is Out",
     "<p>A patio that sits in shade most of the day, whether from a lanai roof, the house itself or mature trees, stays damp longer after each rainy-season storm than an open, sun-exposed slab, and algae or mildew can take hold on a shaded {city} patio within the first year if the finish choice did not account for that exposure. A plain broom finish holds more surface moisture in its texture than a sealed, stained or exposed-aggregate surface does, which makes the finish decision made before the pour part of how the patio ages, not just how it looks on day one. That tradeoff is worth raising at the planning stage, before concrete that cannot easily change finish is already down.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Clearing Joints Around Columns Before the Rain Picks Up",
     "<p>Control joints that run close to a lanai's support columns collect leaves, mulch and wind-blown debris faster than the rest of a {city} patio, partly because planters and furniture tend to cluster near the columns in the first place. Sweeping those joints out ahead of rainy season keeps them doing the job they were cut to do, channeling water instead of trapping it against the edges of each panel, which matters more right around a column where the joint layout is already more complicated than a simple grid. It is a five-minute task with a stiff broom, easy to let slide for a season or two before the debris packs in hard.</p>"),
    ("Algae Finds a Broom Finish Faster Than a Sealed One",
     "<p>A broom finish's texture holds surface moisture longer after rain than a sealed, stained or exposed-aggregate surface does, which is part of why a shaded section of a {city} patio, under a lanai roof or a stand of mature trees, tends to show algae or mildew discoloration before an open, sun-exposed section of the same pour does. Rinsing or lightly pressure washing a shaded patio on a tighter schedule than the sunny parts of the yard, rather than waiting for the whole patio to look dull, keeps that growth from working its way into the surface texture where it is harder to remove later.</p>"),
    ("Checking Where the Downspout Actually Lands Today",
     "<p>A downspout that was aimed fine when the landscaping was last redone can drift or get knocked out of position over the years, and checking where it actually discharges today, not where it was pointed when the {city} patio was poured, is worth doing at least once a year. A head draining straight onto a compacted patio edge wets that same strip of base every time it rains, which works against the base the same way standing water from poor grading would, just more predictably and over a longer stretch of time. Redirecting a drifted downspout costs almost nothing and protects years of base work that is otherwise sound.</p>"),
    ("Timing New Landscaping Around a New Patio's Watering Exemption",
     "<p>New landscaping planted around a freshly poured {city} patio usually qualifies for a temporary watering exemption, daily for the first 30 days and then every other day for the next 30 under the Orlando-area rules, or a similar new-plant schedule under the Sarasota side's current restrictions, rather than the standard once- or twice-a-week limits that apply to established beds. Timing new sod or shrubs around the patio to go in right after the pour, while that exemption window is open, gets new plants established without the irrigation overspray that a fully restricted schedule can leave on fresh concrete edges. Checking which district's rules apply to a specific address is worth doing before the planting date, not after.</p>"),
    ("A Hairline Crack Near a Footer Isn't Automatically a Problem",
     "<p>A hairline crack running along or near a control joint close to a lanai's footer is typically ordinary shrinkage, not a sign the footer itself has a problem, especially on a {city} patio with a few years on it. What is worth a call is a sudden, visible depression near the footer paired with cracking that reaches toward the structure rather than staying in the patio surface, since that combination looks more like a foundation issue than routine settlement. For most homeowners, the practical rule is the same one that applies to any slab: gradual change over months is normal monitoring, and anything abrupt near a structural element is worth a quicker look.</p>"),
    ("Moving the Planters Before They Leave a Mark",
     "<p>A heavy planter, an outdoor kitchen's grill island or a cluster of furniture left in exactly the same spot on a {city} patio for years can leave a faint discoloration or staining ring on the surface, especially on a sealed or stained finish where UV exposure differs between the covered and uncovered concrete underneath. Moving furniture and planters periodically, even just a few inches every season, spreads out that exposure and keeps one section of the patio from visibly aging faster than the rest. It is a small habit that costs nothing and keeps a stained or colored patio looking closer to uniform for longer.</p>"),
    ("What Salt Air Adds to a Coastal Patio's Checklist",
     "<p>A patio within reach of salt air, more of a factor on the coastal side of Sarasota and Manatee than on the Orlando side of the service area, benefits from an occasional check of any exposed rebar or wire mesh near a chip or a crack, since airborne salt speeds up corrosion once metal is exposed to it. Outdoor furniture hardware, a screen enclosure's fasteners and any metal edging near a {city} patio age faster in that same salt air, so a yearly look at the whole setup, not just the concrete, catches small problems before they spread. Coastal exposure does not change how the patio was built, only how closely it is worth watching afterward.</p>"),
    ("Reading the Rain Totals Before Rainy Season Arrives",
     "<p>Orlando averages about 51.45 inches of rain a year and Sarasota-Bradenton about 49.05, most of it falling in a rainy season that typically runs from late spring into October, so walking a {city} patio before that season starts and clearing joints, downspout paths and any low spots is worth doing on a calendar, not just when a problem shows up. A joint packed with sand and leaves, or a downspout that has drifted since the last check, both work against the patio over an entire wet season rather than a single storm. A few minutes of looking in May saves far more time than dealing with a settled corner discovered in August.</p>"),
  ],
}
